FAQ - symptoms with a known cause
=================================

Sorted by what you see, not by what is wrong, because at the point of
asking the second one is not known yet. Each entry says where the full
account lives.


The client boots to a text console instead of a login screen
------------------------------------------------------------

SCREEN_07 is not set to ldm, or the greeter exited at once and is being
respawned. A service that is "active" with NRestarts=0 while burning a
full core is the second case: something in the ldm invocation went wrong
in a way that exits immediately.

Look at /var/log/ldm.log on the client, and at the SCREENS section of
lts.conf(5). HOWTO-lab-from-scratch, step 6, has the whole list of ways
this happens.


A wrong password lands the user in the RDP server's own login window
--------------------------------------------------------------------

Pre-authentication is not running. It is meant to try the credentials over
SSH first and keep a failure inside the greeter, with a message.

RDP_PREAUTH=ssh turns it on, and it needs the server's SSH host key known
under the name RDP_SERVER uses - usually an address, while the image carries
the key under the name "server". RDP_PREAUTH_HOST=server is the quick way
to reconcile that on an image you do not want to rebuild. See the SSH HOST
KEYS section of lts.conf(5).

Raise LDM_LOGLEVEL to 7 to see what pre-authentication asked and what it
heard back.


lts.conf has no effect
----------------------

There are two of them, and the one you edited may not be the one being
read. The client fetches lts.conf over TFTP from the directory its kernel
came from and writes it over /etc/lts.conf in the running client - but only
when the transfer returns a non-empty file. When it fails, and it fails
silently, the client keeps the copy baked into the image.

    ltsp-info

names both paths, says whether each is there, and which of the two wins.
-v prints their contents so they can be compared.


The greeter is in English although LANG says otherwise
------------------------------------------------------

The image does not carry that locale. glibc refuses to translate anything
in the C locale, C.UTF-8 included, and setlocale() failing is not visible
from the outside.

Since ldm 3.4.4-1 the greeter says so in /var/log/ldm.log. Since ltsp
6.3.15-1 an image built without --locale carries the build machine's own
locale, so a German build host gives a German greeter with nothing set.
For a choice between languages, build with

    --locale de_DE.UTF-8,en_US.UTF-8

and let LANG in lts.conf pick. See lts.conf(5), "What language it speaks".


Boot messages show little boxes instead of letters
--------------------------------------------------

The console font is loaded too late, after systemd has started printing.
Fixed in ltsp-client-core 6.3.6-1, which loads it from init-ltsp before
systemd is exec'd; CONSOLE_FONT in lts.conf(5) chooses which one.


The client shuts services down and then stays on
------------------------------------------------

Two different faults with the same appearance. The one in LTSP was
poweroff racing a dead NBD root, fixed in 6.3.4-1 by going through
/proc/sysrq-trigger, which needs no filesystem.

The other is not LTSP's: a virtual machine with no ACPI power-off handler
halts instead of powering down, and says so on the console -
"reboot: Power off not available: System halted instead". Enable ACPI in
the VM.


The image grew by hundreds of megabytes after adding backports
--------------------------------------------------------------

Two causes, and the larger one is avoidable. Debian split the kernel
package after 6.12: what was one 106 MB package is now linux-base plus
linux-binary plus a 153 MB linux-modules, 62 MB more for the same kernel.
That one is the price of the newer kernel.

The other is a firmware metapackage. firmware-linux reaches
firmware-amd-graphics - 93 MB of firmware for AMD graphics chips, on a
client that may have none. Name the package you want:
firmware-intel-graphics is 32 MB. See ltsp-build-client(8).


apt refuses the archive as unsigned
-----------------------------------

The keyring file is missing or the Signed-By line does not point at it.
Do not answer this with [trusted=yes]: that switches verification off for
every package from the source, on machines that install software as root.
The README has the two commands.


